---
import "../../styles/getting-started.css";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import CodeTabs from "../../components/CodeTabs.astro";
import { installTabs, MOJOSHELF_INSTALL } from "../../data/install";
import GettingStartedPager from "../../components/GettingStartedPager.astro";
import {
  GHARCHIVE_REPO,
  SCHEDULER,
  GHARCHIVE_DECL,
  GHARCHIVE_STEP,
  HISTORY_DECL,
  CONSOLE_MAIN,
  CONSOLE_VERSIONS,
} from "../../data/gharchive";

const description =
  "Build a lake of GitHub activity on magmalake by hand: pixi and mojoshelf, a project with Orca in it, the pipelines and the flare console of mojo-census, a local run, and a deploy to AWS with orca aws.";

const SKILLS = "https://github.com/magmalake/skills";
---

<Base title="Step by step — Getting started" description={description}>
  <section class="section section--flush">
    <div class="wrap wrap--wide docs gs">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href="/getting-started">Getting started</a></p>
      <h1 class="section-title">Step by step</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        <a href={GHARCHIVE_REPO} rel="noopener">mojo-census</a> by hand: the tools, a new
        project with Orca in it, the code the agent wrote, excerpted from the repository, and
        how to run it here and on AWS. To run the example as it is instead, clone it, install
        the two tools below, and skip to <a href="#local">Run it locally</a>:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`git clone ${GHARCHIVE_REPO} && cd mojo-census`} />

      <nav class="docs-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#tools">Install pixi and mojoshelf</a>
        <a href="#project">Create the project</a>
        <a href="#code">The code</a>
        <a href="#local">Run it locally</a>
        <a href="#aws">Deploy to AWS</a>
      </nav>

      <h2 id="tools">Install pixi and mojoshelf</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="https://pixi.sh" rel="noopener">pixi</a> manages the project's environment,
        the Mojo compiler included. <a href={MOJOSHELF_INSTALL} rel="noopener">mojoshelf's
        CLI</a> adds <code>pixi shelf</code>, which finds
        and adds tins:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`curl -fsSL https://pixi.sh/install.sh | sh
pixi global install --channel https://mojoshelf.org/channel mojoshelf`} />

      <h2 id="project">Create the project</h2>
      <p>
        A new project needs Modular's channel, pixi's build preview (tins build with
        pixi-build) and the compiler before it adds Orca. The <code>linux-aarch64</code>
        platform is for the image that runs on AWS Graviton instances later:
      </p>
      <CodeTabs label="Install" copy tabs={installTabs("project", `pixi init lake -c https://conda.modular.com/max -c conda-forge && cd lake
sed -i.bak 's/^\\[workspace\\]/[workspace]\\npreview = ["pixi-build"]/' pixi.toml
pixi add "mojo-compiler==1.2.0"
pixi workspace platform add linux-aarch64   # for images on AWS Graviton
pixi shelf add orca-mojo                     # orca-mojo 0.3.2, with dista-mojo, iceberg-mojo, ...`)} />
      <p>
        <code>pixi shelf add orca-mojo</code> brings orca-mojo 0.3.2 and the tins it needs. It
        takes a few minutes and prints every tin's build log as it goes; it hasn't hung.
        mojo-census also adds <code>postgresql</code> and <code>git</code> from
        conda-forge (<code>pixi add postgresql git</code>), for the local database and for
        cloning repositories inside the image. Its layout is the one the magmalake-apps skill
        describes: programs in <code>bin/</code>, one file per pipeline in
        <code>pipelines/</code>, shared code in <code>src/</code>, built with <code>-I src</code>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="code">The code</h2>
      <h3 id="scheduler">The orca command</h3>
      <p>
        The project's <code>orca</code> command, which registers pipelines, runs the
        scheduler and serves the UI, is two lines of its own. Its file must not be named
        <code>orca.mojo</code>, which would hide the <code>orca</code> package:
      </p>
      <p class="docs-file">bin/scheduler.mojo</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={SCHEDULER} />

      <h3 id="gharchive">gharchive</h3>
      <p>
        The gharchive pipeline is one step, mapped over days from 2023-01-01, on a cron
        schedule. The compiler checks the step's input and output types against the pipeline.
        <code>writes=</code> declares the Iceberg table it commits to, and <code>Hour</code> is
        the Dista task that downloads one hour of the archive, gunzips it with deflate-mojo's
        <code>gunzip</code> and parses it:
      </p>
      <p class="docs-file">pipelines/gharchive.mojo</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={GHARCHIVE_DECL} />
      <p>
        The step keeps four hours in flight at a time and appends each one as it comes back,
        so a day of about 15 GB of JSON never sits in memory whole. Each append is a snapshot
        that Orca numbers, so a retried attempt finds the hours its earlier attempt committed
        with <code>find_commit</code> and skips them:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={GHARCHIVE_STEP} />

      <h3 id="mojo-repos">mojo_repos</h3>
      <p>
        mojo_repos is a daily sensor on <code>gharchive.events</code>: each day gharchive
        commits starts a run for that day. It collects candidate repositories from the day's
        events, checks them against GitHub, and appends the Mojo ones to
        <code>mojo.repos</code>, keyed by <code>owner/name</code>. Every answer goes into
        <code>mojo.checked</code>, so a repository is asked about once.
      </p>

      <h3 id="mojo-history">mojo_history</h3>
      <p>
        mojo_history is keyed: its partitions are the <code>repo</code> column of
        <code>mojo.repos</code>, 50 to a run, and as a sensor on that table, each snapshot
        mojo_repos commits starts runs for the repositories it adds. Its one step gathers a
        run's keys and sends every repository to a Dista task that clones it and reads its log:
      </p>
      <p class="docs-file">pipelines/mojo_history.mojo</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={HISTORY_DECL} />
      <p>
        Because the step declares <code>mojo.repos</code> in <code>reads=</code>, Orca's
        lineage runs all the way back: <code>orca lineage mojo.commits</code> traces the
        histories to the <code>mojo.repos</code> snapshot that started them and the
        <code>gharchive.events</code> snapshot and run that one came from.
      </p>

      <h3 id="console">The console</h3>
      <p>
        The console is a <a href="https://github.com/magmalake/flare_mlake" rel="noopener">flare</a>
        app over <code>mojo.repos</code>, <code>mojo.commits</code> and
        <code>mojo.migrations</code>. Each page also answers as JSON under <code>/api/</code>:
      </p>
      <p class="docs-file">bin/console.mojo</p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={CONSOLE_MAIN} />
      <p>
        A route is a function from a request to a response. This one lists, for each month,
        how many libraries moved to each Mojo version, with the versions in one order across
        Modular's renumbering, which <code>tests/test_versions.mojo</code> tests:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="Mojo" code={CONSOLE_VERSIONS} />

      <h2 id="local">Run it locally</h2>
      <p>
        Build everything inside the pixi environment and run the tests. The binaries also run
        from a plain shell, but Postgres and <code>git</code> come from the pixi environment,
        so the commands below use <code>pixi run</code> or <code>pixi shell</code>.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`pixi run build      # build/orca, build/{gharchive,mojo_repos,mojo_history}, build/console
pixi run test       # version ordering and history parsing`} />
      <p>
        The pipelines run on their own without a database. <code>GHARCHIVE_HOURS</code>
        keeps the download to two hourly files of about 100 MB each, and
        <code>MOJO_HISTORY_MAX_KB</code> skips repositories bigger than 100 MB, such as
        modular/modular:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`export ORCA_WAREHOUSE=$PWD/build/warehouse
GHARCHIVE_HOURS=15,17 pixi run ./build/gharchive run --partitions 2024-06-12
pixi run ./build/mojo_repos run --partitions 2024-06-12
MOJO_HISTORY_MAX_KB=100000 pixi run ./build/mojo_history run --partitions @all
PORT=8080 pixi run ./build/console      # http://127.0.0.1:8080`} />
      <p>
        To run them the way they run in production, with the scheduler, the sensors and the
        UI, start a Postgres under <code>build/</code>:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`pixi run initdb -D build/pg --auth=trust --username=postgres -E UTF8 --locale=C
pixi run pg_ctl -D build/pg -l build/pg.log -o "-k $PWD/build -c listen_addresses=" -w start
pixi run createdb -h "$PWD/build" -U postgres orca`} />
      <p>
        <code>scripts/env.sh</code> points <code>ORCA_DATABASE_URL</code> at it, puts the
        warehouse and logs under <code>build/</code> and sets the small slice of the archive.
        Register all three pipelines before loading any data, because a sensor only counts
        snapshots made after it was registered. Then backfill one day:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`pixi shell
. scripts/env.sh
for p in gharchive mojo_repos mojo_history; do ./build/orca locate build/$p --root .; done
./build/orca register ./build/gharchive ./build/mojo_repos ./build/mojo_history
./build/orca scheduler &                 # fires, senses, starts runs
./build/orca ui &                        # http://127.0.0.1:8270 (ORCA_UI_ADDR to move it)
./build/orca backfill gharchive 2024-06-12`} />
      <p>
        The scheduler runs gharchive for the day, which starts mojo_repos for the same day,
        whose new repositories start mojo_history. You can watch it in the UI, or in
        <code>build/logs/&lt;pipeline&gt;/&lt;run&gt;/</code>, and the console from above shows
        the result.
      </p>
      <p>
        Without a token GitHub allows 60 API requests an hour, and mojo_repos spends one to
        three per candidate. On a day with more candidates than that, the rest are recorded as
        unchecked and the next run checks them first. To check them for that day once the
        limit has reset, re-run the day: a plain backfill skips days that already succeeded,
        and <code>--rerun</code> queues them anyway.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./build/orca backfill --rerun mojo_repos 2024-06-12`} />
      <p>
        Set <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> to lift the limit. Re-running a day of gharchive appends
        its events again, since a day's partition isn't overwritten yet. Stop the scheduler and
        the UI with <code>kill</code> and Postgres with
        <code>pixi run pg_ctl -D build/pg stop</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        To change Orca, Iceberg or Parquet and try the change in this project before it is
        released, see <a href="/getting-started/develop">Develop locally</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="aws">Deploy to AWS</h2>
      <p>
        <code>orca aws</code> deploys the project as one CloudFormation stack. Use orca-mojo
        0.3.2 or later: with 0.3.0 and 0.3.1, <code>orca aws push</code> fails to build the
        image on Linux when a pipeline links flare's HTTP client beside dista, as gharchive
        and mojo_repos do. <code>orca aws</code> drives the <code>aws</code> and
        <code>docker</code> commands you already have, so sign in with <code>aws login</code>
        (or set <code>AWS_PROFILE</code>) and have Docker running. The stack's Availability
        Zone defaults to <code>us-east-2a</code>; in another Region pass one of its zones with
        <code>--zone</code>. If you signed up through AWS's new experience, your project's
        Region is in AWS Settings, under View all projects, Overview, Additional Info.
      </p>
      <p>
        Inside <code>pixi shell</code>, create the stack, with the email that gets its budget
        alerts and a hostname each for Orca's UI and the console. Then point an A record for
        each hostname at the <code>PublicAddress</code> that <code>status</code> prints. On
        Cloudflare, set the records to "DNS only", so that Caddy on the instance can get
        certificates. The UI also answers at <code>SslipUrl</code> straight away.
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./build/orca aws up --email you@example.com \\
    --hostname orca.example.com --console-hostname mojo.example.com
./build/orca aws status`} />
      <p>
        Commit, then build the linux-aarch64 image and push it to the stack's registry. Put a
        GitHub token in Secrets Manager under <code>orca/</code>, register the pipelines from
        <code>pipelines/</code> with on-demand workers that scale from none to four while
        tasks wait, give the token to mojo_repos, and start the console on its hostname:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`./build/orca aws push
aws secretsmanager create-secret --name orca/github-token --secret-string "$GITHUB_TOKEN"
./build/orca aws register --workers 0..4 gharchive mojo_history
./build/orca aws register --workers 0..4 --secret GITHUB_TOKEN=orca/github-token mojo_repos
./build/orca aws app console`} />
      <p>
        <code>push</code> builds one image for everything: the five binaries
        (<code>orca</code>, <code>console</code>, <code>gharchive</code>,
        <code>mojo_repos</code> and <code>mojo_history</code>) and the shared libraries they
        link, on <code>debian:bookworm-slim</code>. For mojo-census it is 71.1 MB
        compressed in ECR and 294 MB unpacked.
      </p>
      <p>
        Until orca's next release, two things can trip a first deploy:
      </p>
      <ul class="gs-list">
        <li>
          Right after the first <code>push</code>, the control instance is still starting its
          containers. If <code>register</code> says <code>orca-refresh: command not found</code>
          or <code>No such container: scheduler</code>, wait a minute and run it again.
        </li>
        <li>
          The image needs CA certificates where libcurl looks for them, which is under the
          build environment's prefix, not the system's. Until objectstore reads
          <code>SSL_CERT_FILE</code>, copy mojo-census's
          <a href={`${GHARCHIVE_REPO}/blob/main/Dockerfile.orca`} rel="noopener"><code>Dockerfile.orca</code></a>
          into your project, and <code>push</code> builds it in place of orca's default. It
          is orca's default image plus one line,
          <code>COPY --from=build /src/project/.pixi/envs/default/ssl /src/project/.pixi/envs/default/ssl</code>,
          which keeps the build environment's certificate directory at the same path in the
          final image, where libcurl (objectstore) and OpenSSL (flare) were compiled to look.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        On AWS, <code>GHARCHIVE_HOURS</code> and <code>MOJO_HISTORY_MAX_KB</code> aren't set,
        so runs take all 24 hours of a day and every repository. Runs get the token as a
        variable, and every log Orca writes masks its value. After a code change, commit,
        then run <code>push</code>, <code>register</code> and <code>app console</code> again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The UI is public for reading, and its actions need a login. There's no
        <code>orca aws</code> command for users or backfills yet, so add a user and start the
        first month's backfill on the control instance, through Session Manager. The same
        <code>orca backfill --rerun</code> re-runs a day there:
      </p>
      <CodeBlock label="shell" code={`aws ssm start-session --target <ControlInstance>      # from orca aws status
sudo docker exec -i scheduler orca user add alice        # reads the password from stdin
sudo docker exec scheduler orca backfill gharchive 2025-01-01..2025-01-31`} />
      <p>
        <strong>What it costs.</strong> The control instance, a <code>t4g.large</code> on
        demand with a 40 GB volume and an Elastic IP, runs all month whether or not anything
        runs; it holds Postgres, the scheduler, the UI, Caddy and the console. Each run starts
        on-demand instances of its own, a <code>c7g.large</code> head and
        <code>c7g.xlarge</code> workers unless you register other types, and
        <code>--workers 0..4</code> keeps an idle run at no workers. Downloads from the public
        bucket that holds the lake are billed to you. The stack emails you at 80% of a monthly
        budget, 100 dollars unless you pass <code>--budget</code>. Check the current prices for
        your Region, and look at what the one-month backfill cost before you backfill from
        2023. When you're done, empty the buckets and run
        <code>./build/orca aws down --yes</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/s/orca/docs/">Orca docs</a> cover pipelines, the scheduler and the UI in
        full, and the <a href={`${SKILLS}/blob/main/magmalake-apps/references/aws.md`} rel="noopener">magmalake-apps
        skill's AWS reference</a> lists what the stack holds and what to do when a step of
        the deploy fails.
      </p>

      <GettingStartedPager
        prev={{ href: "/getting-started/agent", label: "Let an agent build it" }}
        next={{ href: "/getting-started/develop", label: "Develop locally" }}
      />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
